    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 292258\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n358 PM PDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Scattered showers and thunderstorms across the eastern half of\n  our forecast area this afternoon and evening.\n\n* Mild weather and warming temperatures are expected during the\n  second half of the week and into early next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Tuesday.\n\nThe upper low is currently near Lake Havasu City and will very \nslowly move to the east this afternoon. Wrap around, mainly light \nshowers are working their way across Clark County. Temperatures \nhave been struggling to increase this morning with all of the \ncloud cover and showers, particularly in the Las Vegas Valley. \nSome clearing should occur during the early afternoon and \ntemperatures should have some recovery. The Flash Flood Watch \ncontinues through this evening for much of Mohave County and by \nmid afternoon expect additional showers and thunderstorms to \ndevelop over central and southern portions of the county where the\ncold pool aloft will be located. This activity will likely linger\nthrough the evening hours before the low pulls away to the east \novernight. The Weather Prediction Center (WPC) has highlighted \nMohave County with a Slight Chance (Level 2 of 4) of excessive \nrainfall capable of resulting in flash flooding. Meanwhile WPC has\nhighlighted portions of Clark, Lincoln, and San Bernardino \nCounties with a Marginal Chance (Level 1 of 4) of excessive \nrainfall capable of resulting in flash flooding. Outside of \nmoderate-to-heavy rain, small hail, lightning, and gusty outflow \nwinds will be possible with any storms that develop. \n\nA ridge of high pressure will build into the region on Wednesday\nand will be the main player into the weekend. Temperatures will \nbe 3 to 6 degrees above their seasonal norms through the period. \nThe ensembles are the potential for a weak shortwave to move \nonshore along the central California coast on Sunday. At this \npoint, it may provide an increase in winds and some cloud cover. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 00Z Forecast\nPackage...Convective activity has waned across the Las Vegas \nValley this afternoon, though there remains potential for passing \nshowers to impact the Valley through the first 1-2 hours of the \nforecast period. These showers will remain light, with minimal \nimpacts expected if one passes over the terminal. Ceilings will \nimprove overnight, clearing by mid-morning. Elevated and \nintermittently gusty northwesterly to northerly winds will \ncontinue through sunset, becoming light and somewhat variable \novernight. Tuesday morning, another round of gusty northwesterly \nwinds is expected, before gusts diminish and wind speeds drop \nunder 10KT by early afternoon.\n\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Scattered showers and\nthunderstorms continue moving westward across central and \nsouthern Mohave County, expected to impact the Colorado River \nValley, including KIFP and KEED, through mid-evening. These storms\nwill produce heavy rain resulting in transient MVFR/IFR \nconditions, along with erratic gusty winds and frequent lightning.\nOutside of convective influences, gusty northerly winds will \ntaper off through the evening, becoming lighter overnight for most\nand with lingering northerly flow on Wednesday. The main exception \nwill be KDAG/the western Mojave, where winds return to the west \novernight through the end of the forecast period. Ceilings around \n8-10kft could be lower with passing showers and storms, with \nimprovement expected overnight and skies clearing areawide by late\nmorning into early afternoon.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Czyzyk\nAVIATION...Phillipson\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
